    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Monocytes circulate the bloodstream and are important to the immune system because they differentiate into macrophages and dendritic cells. Traditionally, monocytes were identified as two populations using CD14 and CD16 surface markers: classical monocytes are CD14+ and nonclassical monocytes are CD16+. However, a third intermediate monocyte has been described and these intermediate monocytes express both CD14+ and CD16+. The role of these intermediate monocytes is not well known, but the expression of HLA-DR, an MHC class II molecule, is known to be highest in the intermediate monocytes. To further understand the role of these intermediate monocytes, flow cytometry was used to look at the expression of HLA-DM and HLA-DP at the protein level across the different monocyte subsets. HLA-DM and HLA-DP were most highly expressed by the intermediate monocytes, suggesting they can load foreign antigen peptide onto class II molecules the most. To learn more about the differential antigen presentation capacity across the monocyte subsets, surface CLIP, a placeholder peptide in the binding groove of HLA class II molecules, was analyzed. Ratios of CLIP/DR were determined because they show how much CLIP makes it to the surface of the cell on the class II molecule without being replaced by foreign antigen peptides, a function performed by HLA-DM. The intermediate monocytes showed the lowest CLIP/DR ratios as expected, further showing that the intermediate monocytes possess the strongest antigen presentation capacity amongst the monocytes. Additionally, an antibody known to bind against HLA-DR1/CLIP, HLA-DR3/CLIP, and HLA-DR4/CLIP complexes (14-23) was used to calculate 14-23/DR ratios to provide more specific functional information about CLIP accumulation on the cell surface. The 14-23/DR ratios were calculated and found to be the lowest in the intermediate monocytes, which correlates with our finding of high expression of HLA-DM in the intermediate monocytes. Overall, our data support the accumulating evidence that human intermediate monocytes possess the highest antigen presentation capacity through elevated expression of class II pathway molecules. This information is vital in helping determine the role of intermediate monocytes, which are implicated in many diseases, including rheumatoid arthritis, coronary artery disease, and HIV.",
